Azure VNET Inventory PowerShell Script
Overview

This PowerShell script collects Azure Virtual Network (VNET) inventory information from multiple Azure subscriptions.

The script reads a CSV file containing the Subscription ID and Subscription Name, connects to each subscription, retrieves all VNETs, and exports the collected information to a CSV file.

The generated report contains:

Subscription ID
Subscription Name
VNET Name
Resource Group
Azure Region / Location
VNET Address Space
Subnet Name
Subnet Address Range

Each subnet is reported as a separate row in the output CSV.

Prerequisites

Before running the script, make sure the following requirements are met.

1. PowerShell

The script can be run using:

Windows PowerShell 5.1
PowerShell 7.x

PowerShell 7.x is recommended.

2. Azure PowerShell Module

The script requires the following Azure PowerShell modules:

Az.Accounts
Az.Network

To install the complete Az module:

Install-Module Az -Scope CurrentUser


Or install only the required modules:

Install-Module Az.Accounts -Scope CurrentUser
Install-Module Az.Network -Scope CurrentUser


To verify the installed Az version:

Get-InstalledModule Az

Azure Permissions

The Azure account used to execute the script must have permission to read network resources in the target subscriptions.

At minimum, the account should have read access to:

Virtual Networks
Subnets
Resource Groups

A role such as Reader at the subscription level is generally sufficient for collecting this inventory.

The user/service principal must have access to all subscriptions listed in the input CSV.

Input CSV File

The script expects a CSV file containing the Azure Subscription ID and Subscription Name.

Example

Create a file named:

subscriptions.csv


Example contents:

SubscriptionId,SubscriptionName
xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx,Production
yyyyyyyy-yyyy-yyyy-yyyy-yyyyyyyyyyyy,Development
zzzzzzzz-zzzz-zzzz-zzzz-zzzzzzzzzzzz,Test

CSV Columns
Column	Description	Required
SubscriptionId	Azure Subscription ID	Yes
SubscriptionName	Azure Subscription Name	Yes

The column names must match the names expected by the script.

Script Configuration

The script uses the following input and output file paths:

$CsvPath = ".\subscriptions.csv"
$OutputCsv = ".\Azure-VNET-Inventory.csv"


You can modify these paths if required.

For example:

$CsvPath = "C:\Azure\Input\subscriptions.csv"
$OutputCsv = "C:\Azure\Output\Azure-VNET-Inventory.csv"

How to Run
Step 1: Save the Script

Save the PowerShell script as:

Get-AzureVNETInventory.ps1


Place the script and CSV file in the same directory:

Azure-VNET-Inventory\
│
├── Get-AzureVNETInventory.ps1
└── subscriptions.csv

Step 2: Login to Azure

The script performs an interactive Azure login using:

Connect-AzAccount


Run the script:

.\Get-AzureVNETInventory.ps1


You will be prompted to authenticate to Azure.

Step 3: Select the Required Azure Account

If your account has access to multiple Azure tenants, you may need to select the appropriate tenant during authentication.

Step 4: Wait for Processing

The script processes each subscription from the CSV.

Example console output:

