How the Script Works

The script performs the following operations:

Imports the subscription list from the CSV file.
Connects to Azure using Connect-AzAccount.
Reads each subscription from the CSV.
Sets the Azure subscription context using Set-AzContext.
Retrieves all VNETs using Get-AzVirtualNetwork.
Extracts:
VNET name
Resource group
Location
Address space
Enumerates all subnets within each VNET.
Extracts the subnet name and address range.
Creates an inventory object for each subnet.
Exports the complete inventory to a CSV file.
Continues processing other subscriptions if an individual subscription encounters an error.
Error Handling

The script uses a try/catch block when processing each subscription.

If a subscription cannot be accessed or an error occurs, the script displays a warning similar to:

WARNING: Failed to process subscription Production
[xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx].
Error: The client does not have authorization...


The script then continues processing the remaining subscriptions.

This prevents a single inaccessible subscription from stopping the entire inventory process.

Important Considerations
Subscription Access

The subscription must be accessible by the Azure account used for authentication.

You can verify the subscriptions available to your account with:

Get-AzSubscription

Subscription ID Accuracy

Ensure that the SubscriptionId values in the CSV are valid Azure subscription IDs.

Subscription Name

The SubscriptionName is primarily used for reporting. The script uses the SubscriptionId to set the Azure subscription context.

VNETs Without Subnets

If a VNET does not contain any subnets, the script still includes the VNET in the output. The subnet-related fields will be blank.

Multiple Address Spaces

A VNET can contain multiple address spaces. These are combined into the AddressSpace column and separated by semicolons.

For example:

10.10.0.0/16; 10.20.0.0/16

Troubleshooting
Error: Get-AzVirtualNetwork is not recognized

Install the Azure Network module:

Install-Module Az.Network -Scope CurrentUser


Then import it:

Import-Module Az.Network

Error: Connect-AzAccount is not recognized

Install the Azure Accounts module:

Install-Module Az.Accounts -Scope CurrentUser


Then try:

Connect-AzAccount

Error: Authorization Failed

Verify that your Azure account has at least read access to the subscription and its network resources.

Check the current context:

Get-AzContext


Check available subscriptions:

Get-AzSubscription

Script Cannot Run Because Script Execution Is Disabled

If PowerShell blocks the script, check the execution policy:

Get-ExecutionPolicy


If permitted by your organization's security policy, you can run:

Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned


Then execute:

.\Get-AzureVNETInventory.ps1

